{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0161WS3Wb63D39yHpb2Yfuw6","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform overall assessment soundness excitement scoring\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"},{\"title\":\"Response to ACL 2023 Questions\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/\"},{\"title\":\"Incentives 2026\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/incentives2026\"},{\"title\":\"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Changelog\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/changelog\"},{\"title\":\"Action Editor Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/actioneditorform\"}]\n\nBased on the search results from the ACL Rolling Review website, here's information about the review form scoring for overall assessment, soundness, and excitement:\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of a paper if it were committed to an *ACL venue, reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact.\n\n## Soundness Scoring\n\nReviewers assess whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, clearly states scientific claims and provides adequate support for them. The soundness scale ranges from 5 = Excellent (one of the most thorough studies) to 4 = Strong (providing sufficient support for all claims) to 3 = Acceptable (providing sufficient support for main claims). Soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review, and if a low soundness score is given without finding major faults, the review needs to be revised.\n\n## Excitement Scoring\n\nThe excitement question asks how exciting the paper is to the reviewer, noting that excitement is subjective and does not necessarily follow what is popular in the field. The scale ranges from 5 = Highly Exciting (recommending the paper to others or attending its presentation) to 4 = Exciting (mentioning the paper to others or making an effort to attend its presentation). Excitement scores reflect personal taste and do not necessarily come with explicit reasons.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2bc5c33-59bf-4c89-8347-ed0dbc0c532c","uuid":"29a0f5ee-1707-42b6-9e4a-615749853615","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:42:02.689Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform overall assessment soundness excitement scoring","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01BFVKTvQtfLMzdXsWV69NJU","content":[{"title":"Review Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"},{"title":"Response to ACL 2023 Questions","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/"},{"title":"Incentives 2026","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/incentives2026"},{"title":"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"ARR Changelog","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/changelog"},{"title":"Action Editor Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/actioneditorform"}]},"Based on the search results from the ACL Rolling Review website, here's information about the review form scoring for overall assessment, soundness, and excitement:\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of a paper if it were committed to an *ACL venue, reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact.\n\n## Soundness Scoring\n\nReviewers assess whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, clearly states scientific claims and provides adequate support for them. The soundness scale ranges from 5 = Excellent (one of the most thorough studies) to 4 = Strong (providing sufficient support for all claims) to 3 = Acceptable (providing sufficient support for main claims). Soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review, and if a low soundness score is given without finding major faults, the review needs to be revised.\n\n## Excitement Scoring\n\nThe excitement question asks how exciting the paper is to the reviewer, noting that excitement is subjective and does not necessarily follow what is popular in the field. The scale ranges from 5 = Highly Exciting (recommending the paper to others or attending its presentation) to 4 = Exciting (mentioning the paper to others or making an effort to attend its presentation). Excitement scores reflect personal taste and do not necessarily come with explicit reasons."],"durationSeconds":6.788974076999999}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018JB8srNdwAZESo5a6Zkpfx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have enough context about the EMNLP 2026 / ARR review form. Let me compile the key information:\n\n**ARR/EMNLP Review Form (2025-2026):**\n- Three scores: Soundness, Excitement, Overall Assessment\n- Scale: 1-5 (with half-point increments)\n- Overall Assessment: 5 = Award-worthy (~top 2.5%), 4.5 = Strong accept, 4 = Accept, 3.5 = Leaning accept, 3 = Ambivalent/borderline, 2.5 = Leaning reject, 2 = Resubmit next cycle, 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle, 1 = Do not resubmit\n\n\n- Soundness ranges from 5 (Excellent) to 1 (Major issues), and Excitement from 5 (Highly exciting) to 1 (Not exciting)\n\nNow I'm setting up the full review process with two specialized reviewers—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—followed by a structured roundtable discussion moving from independent assessments through cross-sharing and toward consensus.\n\nI'm dispatching both reviewers in parallel to evaluate this paper on phase-gated compound sparsity, which examines how head pruning, KV cache eviction, and reasoning phases interact during LLM inference. The key findings show that importance-based eviction creates strong compound interactions with measurable phase gradients, while random eviction shows minimal phase effects, with cross-architecture validation on Llama confirming the results hold across models. The discrimination collapse metric quantitatively predicts interaction severity with strong correlation.\n\nAll six planned experiments appear to be covered in the paper—including the Llama cross-architecture validation, GPQA importance profiling, random baselines with strategy specificity analysis, Gini coefficient correlation, and the related work sections.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":754,"cache_read_input_tokens":56694,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":754},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2bc5c33-59bf-4c89-8347-ed0dbc0c532c","uuid":"824f3d2c-5897-48b2-b40d-545782cf81e0"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018JB8srNdwAZESo5a6Zkpfx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the paper content, review context, and EMNLP/ARR review criteria. Now dispatching two parallel blind reviewers: R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":754,"cache_read_input_tokens":56694,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":754},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2bc5c33-59bf-4c89-8347-ed0dbc0c532c","uuid":"cee60d51-9e52-4d46-be18-8819f447c865"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018JB8srNdwAZESo5a6Zkpfx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01L3oMjxcWGpyyNSRHwEBtxY","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission, focusing on **methods and experiments**. This uses ACL Rolling Review (ARR). You must provide a blind, independent review.\n\n## ARR Scoring Rubric\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent (most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Poor, 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Ambivalent, 2=Below average, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5, half-point increments): 5=Award-worthy (top 2.5%), 4=Accept, 3.5=Leaning accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning reject, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- **Reproducibility** (1-5): Can the community reproduce the results?\n\n## Paper: \"Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity: Strategy×Phase×Compound Interaction in Efficient LLM Inference\"\n\n### Abstract\nCompound sparsity—simultaneous attention head pruning and KV cache eviction—is increasingly adopted for efficient LLM inference. The paper discovers that compound interaction severity is not fixed but an emergent function of eviction strategy and reasoning phase. Experiments on 3 models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B), 2 architectures (Qwen2, Llama), and 3 benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA-Diamond) show importance-based eviction has monotonically increasing phase gradient (0.39→8.48 on MATH-500) while random eviction is negligible. Strategy specificity ratio ≥5× on GPQA, 15–36× on MATH-500. Cross-architecture validation at n=250 confirms generalization (dz=0.52, Bonferroni p&lt;0.001). Discrimination collapse (Gini coefficient) predicts interaction severity (r=−0.86, p=0.003).\n\n### Method (§3)\n- **Models**: DS-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DS-R1-Distill-Llama-8B\n- **Benchmarks**: MATH-500 (n=500), GSM8K (n=50), GPQA-Diamond (n=50)\n- **Eviction strategies**: Importance-based (lowest attention-weighted scores) vs Random (control)\n- **Sparsity levels**: s ∈ {0.015, 0.020, 0.025, 0.030} for head pruning\n- **Metrics**: Interaction Index (II) = Δ_compound − (Δ_pruning + Δ_eviction); Phase Gradient = median(II_low − II_high); Cohen's dz with 10K bootstrap CI + Bonferroni correction (k=4); Strategy Specificity Ratio (SSR) = imp_gradient / rand_gradient\n- **Mechanism**: Gini coefficient of importance score distributions; prediction: |ΔGini| correlates with II\n\n### Experiments (§4)\n**§4.1 Cross-Architecture (LLaMA n=250)**\n- Table 1: imp_vs_rand dz monotonically increases: 0.25→0.34→0.44→0.52; 3/4 Bonferroni significant\n- Table 2: Random phase gradient attenuates from n=50 (dz=0.35-0.44) to n=250 (dz=0.14-0.22), none Bonferroni significant. Resolves \"architecture-dependent\" caveat.\n- Cross-arch: DS Qwen2 dz=0.80 vs LLaMA dz=0.52 (Δ=0.28). Direction same, magnitude differs.\n- Importance gradient stable: dz=0.95-1.12 at n=250\n\n**§4.2 Cross-Benchmark (GPQA-Diamond)**\n- Table 3: DS importance gradient 0.19→0.58→1.72→4.15 (ρ=1.0), all 4 Bonferroni sig. QW: 0.038→0.19→0.85→2.28 (ρ=1.0), 3/4 Bonferroni sig.\n- Magnitude ordering: MATH > GPQA > GSM8K (reflects reasoning chain complexity)\n\n**§4.3 Strategy Specificity**\n- Table 4: GPQA imp/rand ratio 6.3-8.1× (all >5×). Random gradients weak and mostly NS.\n- Table 5: Cross-benchmark specificity: MATH 15-36×, GPQA 6.3-8.1×, GSM8K 2.3-3.8×\n\n**§4.4 Discrimination Collapse**\n- Table 6: Within-model Spearman ρ=−1.0 for both DS and QW; Pearson r=−0.94 (DS), −0.96 (QW)\n- Cross-model pooled (n=8): Spearman ρ=−0.90, Pearson r=−0.86, p=0.003\n- Gini delta: DS [−0.016 to −0.037] → II [0.39 to 8.48]; QW [−0.017 to −0.035] → II [0.066 to 3.155]\n\n**§4.5 Claim Status**: C1, C2, C5, C8, C10 confirmed; C4 partially confirmed\n\n**Limitations** (§5): (1) 7B-scale only; (2) specificity task-dependent; (3) mechanism correlational not causal; (4) GPQA/GSM8K n=50\n\n### Review Context (imagined experiments from design)\nThe experimental design specified 6 experiments:\n1. LLaMA n=250 cross-arch validation → covered in §4.1\n2. GPQA DS importance profiling → covered in §4.2\n3. GPQA QW importance cross-model → covered in §4.2\n4. GPQA random baselines + strategy specificity → covered in §4.3\n5. Gini-II correlation analysis → covered in §4.4\n6. C8 status upgrade + C9 related work → covered in §4.5 and §2\n\nAll 6 experiments appear represented in the paper.\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Experimental design soundness**: Are controls adequate? Is the factorial design appropriate? Are sample sizes sufficient?\n2. **Statistical methodology**: Bootstrap CI, Bonferroni correction, effect size reporting—rigorous or flawed?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Are details sufficient to replicate? Code/data availability?\n4. **Internal validity**: Could confounds explain the results? Is the interaction index well-defined?\n5. **Experiment coverage**: Are the experiments complete relative to the claims?\n6. **Limitations acknowledgment**: Are they honest and complete?\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted)\n- **Questions for Authors** (bulleted)\n- **Scores**: Soundness, Excitement, Overall Assessment, Reproducibility (with brief justification)\n- **Confidence**: 1-5 (5=expert, 4=high, 3=medium, 2=low, 1=outsider)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is EMNLP—expect strong empirical NLP work."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":754,"cache_read_input_tokens":56694,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":754},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2bc5c33-59bf-4c89-8347-ed0dbc0c532c","uuid":"933aa2f3-e7c6-4909-9321-5e01af8af4e1"}
