marks**: MATH-500 (n=500), GSM8K (n=50), GPQA-Diamond (n=50)\n- **Eviction strategies**: Importance-based (lowest attention-weighted scores) vs Random (control)\n- **Sparsity levels**: s ∈ {0.015, 0.020, 0.025, 0.030} for head pruning\n- **Metrics**: Interaction Index (II) = Δ_compound − (Δ_pruning + Δ_eviction); Phase Gradient = median(II_low − II_high); Cohen's dz with 10K bootstrap CI + Bonferroni correction (k=4); Strategy Specificity Ratio (SSR) = imp_gradient / rand_gradient\n- **Mechanism**: Gini coefficient of importance score distributions; prediction: |ΔGini| correlates with II\n\n### Experiments (§4)\n**§4.1 Cross-Architecture (LLaMA n=250)**\n- Table 1: imp_vs_rand dz monotonically increases: 0.25→0.34→0.44→0.52; 3/4 Bonferroni significant\n- Table 2: Random phase gradient attenuates from n=50 (dz=0.35-0.44) to n=250 (dz=0.14-0.22), none Bonferroni significant. Resolves \"architecture-dependent\" caveat.\n- Cross-arch: DS Qwen2 dz=0.80 vs LLaMA dz=0.52 (Δ=0.28). Direction same, magnitude differs.\n- Importance gradient stable: dz=0.95-1.12 at n=250\n\n**§4.2 Cross-Benchmark (GPQA-Diamond)**\n- Table 3: DS importance gradient 0.19→0.58→1.72→4.15 (ρ=1.0), all 4 Bonferroni sig. QW: 0.038→0.19→0.85→2.28 (ρ=1.0), 3/4 Bonferroni sig.\n- Magnitude ordering: MATH > GPQA > GSM8K (reflects reasoning chain complexity)\n\n**§4.3 Strategy Specificity**\n- Table 4: GPQA imp/rand ratio 6.3-8.1× (all >5×). Random gradients weak and mostly NS.\n- Table 5: Cross-benchmark specificity: MATH 15-36×, GPQA 6.3-8.1×, GSM8K 2.3-3.8×\n\n**§4.4 Discrimination Collapse**\n- Table 6: Within-model Spearman ρ=−1.0 for both DS and QW; Pearson r=−0.94 (DS), −0.96 (QW)\n- Cross-model pooled (n=8): Spearman ρ=−0.90, Pearson r=−0.86, p=0.003\n- Gini delta: DS [−0.016 to −0.037] → II [0.39 to 8.48]; QW [−0.017 to −0.035] → II [0.066 to 3.155]\n\n**§4.5 Claim Status**: C1, C2, C5, C8, C10 confirmed; C4 partially confirmed\n\n**Limitations** (§5): (1) 7B-scale only; (2) specificity task-dependent; (3) mechanism correlational not causal; (4) GPQA/GSM8K n=50\n\n### Review Context (imagined experiments from design)\nThe experimental design specified 6 experiments:\n1. LLaMA n=250 cross-arch validation → covered in §4.1\n2. GPQA DS importance profiling → covered in §4.2\n3. GPQA QW importance cross-model → covered in §4.2\n4. GPQA random baselines + strategy specificity → covered in §4.3\n5. Gini-II correlation analysis → covered in §4.4\n6. C8 status upgrade + C9 related work → covered in §4.5 and §2\n\nAll 6 experiments appear represented in the paper.\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Experimental design soundness**: Are controls adequate? Is the factorial design appropriate? Are sample sizes sufficient?\n2. **Statistical methodology**: Bootstrap CI, Bonferroni correction, effect size reporting—rigorous or flawed?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Are details sufficient to replicate? Code/data availability?\n4. **Internal validity**: Could confounds explain the results? Is the interaction index well-defined?\n5. **Experiment coverage**: Are the experiments complete relative to the claims?\n6. **Limitations acknowledgment**: Are they honest and complete?\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted)\n- **Questions for Authors** (bulleted)\n- **Scores**: Soundness, Excitement, Overall Assessment, Reproducibility (with brief justification)\n- **Confidence**: 1-5 (5=expert, 4=high, 3=medium, 2=low, 1=outsider)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is EMNLP—expect strong empirical NLP work.","uuid":"81f01c30-8b86-4a4c-b6cc-e53485e1e3ae","session_id":"b2bc5c33-59bf-4c89-8347-ed0dbc0c532c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission, focusing on **methods and experiments**. This uses ACL Rolling Review (ARR). You must provide a blind, independent review.\n\n## ARR Scoring Rubric\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent (most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Poor, 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Ambivalent, 2=Below average, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5, half-point increments): 5=Award-worthy (top 2.5%), 4=Accept, 3.5=Leaning accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning reject, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- **Reproducibility** (1-5): Can the community reproduce the results?\n\n## Paper: \"Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity: Strategy×Phase×Compound Interaction in Efficient LLM Inference\"\n\n### Abstract\nCompound sparsity—simultaneous attention head pruning and KV cache eviction—is increasingly adopted for efficient LLM inference. The paper discovers that compound interaction severity is not fixed but an emergent function of eviction strategy and reasoning phase. Experiments on 3 models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B), 2 architectures (Qwen2, Llama), and 3 benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA-Diamond) show importance-based eviction has monotonically increasing phase gradient (0.39→8.48 on MATH-500) while random eviction is negligible. Strategy specificity ratio ≥5× on GPQA, 15–36× on MATH-500. Cross-architecture validation at n=250 confirms generalization (dz=0.52, Bonferroni p&lt;0.001). Discrimination collapse (Gini coefficient) predicts interaction severity (r=−0.86, p=0.003).\n\n### Method (§3)\n- **Models**: DS-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DS-R1-Distill-Llama-8B\n- **Benchmarks**: MATH-500 (n=500), GSM8K (n=50), GPQA-Diamond (n=50)\n- **Eviction strategies**: Importance-based (lowest attention-weighted scores) vs Random (control)\n- **Sparsity levels**: s ∈ {0.015, 0.020, 0.025, 0.030} for head pruning\n- **Metrics**: Interaction Index (II) = Δ_compound − (Δ_pruning + Δ_eviction); Phase Gradient = median(II_low − II_high); Cohen's dz with 10K bootstrap CI + Bonferroni correction (k=4); Strategy Specificity Ratio (SSR) = imp_gradient / rand_gradient\n- **Mechanism**: Gini coefficient of importance score distributions; prediction: |ΔGini| correlates with II\n\n### Experiments (§4)\n**§4.1 Cross-Architecture (LLaMA n=250)**\n- Table 1: imp_vs_rand dz monotonically increases: 0.25→0.34→0.44→0.52; 3/4 Bonferroni significant\n- Table 2: Random phase gradient attenuates from n=50 (dz=0.35-0.44) to n=250 (dz=0.14-0.22), none Bonferroni significant. Resolves \"architecture-dependent\" caveat.\n- Cross-arch: DS Qwen2 dz=0.80 vs LLaMA dz=0.52 (Δ=0.28). Direction same, magnitude differs.\n- Importance gradient stable: dz=0.95-1.12 at n=250\n\n**§4.2 Cross-Benchmark (GPQA-Diamond)**\n- Table 3: DS importance gradient 0.19→0.58→1.72→4.15 (ρ=1.0), all 4 Bonferroni sig. QW: 0.038→0.19→0.85→2.28 (ρ=1.0), 3/4 Bonferroni sig.\n- Magnitude ordering: MATH > GPQA > GSM8K (reflects reasoning chain complexity)\n\n**§4.3 Strategy Specificity**\n- Table 4: GPQA imp/rand ratio 6.3-8.1× (all >5×). Random gradients weak and mostly NS.\n- Table 5: Cross-benchmark specificity: MATH 15-36×, GPQA 6.3-8.1×, GSM8K 2.3-3.8×\n\n**§4.4 Discrimination Collapse**\n- Table 6: Within-model Spearman ρ=−1.0 for both DS and QW; Pearson r=−0.94 (DS), −0.96 (QW)\n- Cross-model pooled (n=8): Spearman ρ=−0.90, Pearson r=−0.86, p=0.003\n- Gini delta: DS [−0.016 to −0.037] → II [0.39 to 8.48]; QW [−0.017 to −0.035] → II [0.066 to 3.155]\n\n**§4.5 Claim Status**: C1, C2, C5, C8, C10 confirmed; C4 partially confirmed\n\n**Limitations** (§5): (1) 7B-scale only; (2) specificity task-dependent; (3) mechanism correlational not causal; (4) GPQA/GSM8K n=50\n\n### Review Context (imagined experiments from design)\nThe experimental design specified 6 experiments:\n1. LLaMA n=250 cross-arch validation → covered in §4.1\n2. GPQA DS importance profiling → covered in §4.2\n3. GPQA QW importance cross-model → covered in §4.2\n4. GPQA random baselines + strategy specificity → covered in §4.3\n5. Gini-II correlation analysis → covered in §4.4\n6. C8 status upgrade + C9 related work → covered in §4.5 and §2\n\nAll 6 experiments appear represented in the paper.\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Experimental design soundness**: Are controls adequate? Is the factorial design appropriate? Are sample sizes sufficient?\n2. **Statistical methodology**: Bootstrap CI, Bonferroni correction, effect size reporting—rigorous or flawed?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Are details sufficient to replicate? Code/data availability?\n4. **Internal validity**: Could confounds explain the results? Is the interaction index well-defined?\n5. **Experiment coverage**: Are the experiments complete relative to the claims?\n6. **Limitations acknowledgment**: Are they honest and complete?\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted)\n- **Questions for Authors** (bulleted)\n- **Scores**: Soundness, Excitement, Overall Assessment, Reproducibility (with brief justification)\n- **Confidence**: 1-5 (5=expert, 4=high, 3=medium, 2=low, 1=outsider)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is EMNLP—expect strong empirical NLP work."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01L3oMjxcWGpyyNSRHwEBtxY","session_id":"b2bc5c33-59bf-4c89-8347-ed0dbc0c532c","uuid":"70a49dc2-718f-4a25-889e-707262d0d429","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:42:59.985Z"}
